WHY IT EXISTS

The wake word runs on Espressif's `esp-sr` (WakeNet), and `esp-sr` is not
under the ordinary MIT licence. It is under the "ESPRESSIF MIT License",
whose grant begins:

    Permission is hereby granted for use on all ESPRESSIF SYSTEMS
    products, in which case, it is free of charge, to any person
    obtaining a copy of this software ...

That clause restricts the field of use to Espressif hardware. It is a
restriction the GPL does not permit to be imposed on a recipient, so
without this exception a binary combining our GPL code with `esp-sr` —
a release asset, or an image served by a web installer — would carry two
sets of terms that contradict each other. The person receiving it would
have no way to obey both.

Handing someone a GPL-licensed binary and then telling them it may only
run on one manufacturer's chips is not a licence, it is a puzzle. This
paragraph removes the contradiction, and it costs the recipient nothing:
every freedom the GPL gives them over our code is untouched, and the only
thing added is permission to combine it with the vendor SDK the hardware
needs — which is the combination the program exists to be.

Publishing our source has never had this problem; the SDK is fetched by
the component manager and is not redistributed here. The exception is
about the binaries, and about anyone who forks this and ships one.

If Espressif's terms ever become unacceptable to you, the wake word is
the only thing that depends on them: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` records
microWakeWord on TFLM (Apache-2.0, trainable by us on the host) as the
free replacement for WakeNet.

